ize:12pt\"><span>In the context of constituents of Earth crust, which of the following is NOT the type of Rocks?</span></p>","option_1":"Igneous","option_2":"Sedimentary","option_3":"Metamorphic","option_4":"Caldera","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Caldera.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic are the three main classifications of rocks based on their mode of formation. A Caldera, by contrast, is a large volcanic depression or crater formed after a volcanic eruption or collapse, not a rock type.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31285","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"840","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the term for the chemical process in which a substance reacts with oxygen to give off heat?</span></p>","option_1":"Oxidation","option_2":"Reduction","option_3":"Combustion","option_4":"Sublimation","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Combustion.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Combustion is an exothermic chemical process in which a substance reacts rapidly with oxygen, releasing heat and often light, as seen in burning reactions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31286","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"840","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is an example of Public Good?</span></p>","option_1":"National defence","option_2":"Clothes","option_3":"Cars","option_4":"Food items","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - National defence.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>National defence is a classic example of a pure public good, as it is non-excludable (everyone in the country benefits) and non-rivalrous (one person's benefit does not reduce availability to others), unlike private goods such as clothes, cars, or food.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31287","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"840","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The budgetary deficit is financed by which of the following?</span></p>","option_1":"Only by taxation","option_2":"Only by borrowing","option_3":"Only by printing money","option_4":"Either by taxation, borrowing or printing money","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Either by taxation, borrowing or printing money.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A budgetary (fiscal) deficit can be financed through multiple means - by raising additional taxation, borrowing from the market or external sources, or printing new currency (deficit financing), depending on the government's fiscal policy choices.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31288","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"840","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Semicon India Programme was launched in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"1999","option_2":"2001","option_3":"2011","option_4":"2021","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - 2021.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Semicon India Programme was launched by the Government of India in December 2021, with an outlay to develop a sustainable semiconductor and display ecosystem in the country.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_